ACE inhibitor-induced cough
Cardiovascular
Persistent dry cough, Onset after ACE inhibitor, Normal chest radiograph, Resolution upon drug cessation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-002
Reason for Visit: Distressing, persistent nocturnal cough preventing sleep and causing moderate respiratory exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a relentless, severe dry cough preventing sleep, with the patient reporting mild chest wall pain from coughing spasms. Assessment was required to urgently rule out acute pulmonary edema, atypical pneumonia, or significant airway obstruction. Evaluation could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period due to respiratory distress and the need to establish a definitive diagnosis and provide immediate symptomatic relief.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have this non-stop, tickly dry cough that gets worse as soon as I lie down, and it's keeping me awake all night."
History of Present Illness: Persistent dry hacking cough commenced approximately three weeks ago, shortly after initiating a new antihypertensive medication. Patient notes a tickling or scratching sensation in the posterior pharynx. Exacerbation of cough when lying supine. Absence of copious sputum production. Lack of response to standard over-the-counter antitussive medications. KEY SYMPTOMS: Persistent dry hacking cough. Onset of cough typically within weeks of initiating angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor therapy. Absence of copious sputum production. Tickling or scratching sensation in the posterior pharynx. Exacerbation of cough when lying supine. Lack of response to standard antitussive medications.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently fatigued due to severe sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, exhaustion increasing fall risk overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Fatigued, speaking in full sentences but interrupted by hacking cough.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Normal pulmonary auscultation without wheeze or crackles. Good bilateral air entry. Cardiovascular: Dual heart sounds, no murmurs, no peripheral edema. ENT: Mild posterior pharyngeal erythema consistent with mechanical irritation, no exudate. Absence of fever or systemic inflammatory signs. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Persistent dry cough, Onset after ACE inhibitor, Normal chest radiograph (presumed, to be ordered), Resolution upon drug cessation (planned).
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty over-the-counter cough syrup bottles on nightstand. Heating adequate.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against electronic health record. Recently commenced Perindopril four weeks ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: ACE inhibitor-induced cough - Status: Stable but highly symptomatic
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Edema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bibasal crackles, normal oxygen saturation, and lack of elevated jugular venous pressure.

Atypical Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal auscultation, and no systemic inflammatory signs.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dyspepsia, waterbrash, and specific correlation with recent medication change.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, unrelenting cough causing sleep deprivation and respiratory exhaustion, warranting prompt clinical exclusion of acute cardiopulmonary failure. Objective examination revealed clear lung fields, normal oxygenation, and an absence of infectious signs, pointing directly to an iatrogenic etiology given the recent commencement of an ACE inhibitor. The severity of the distress and the need for urgent physical examination to rule out acute pulmonary pathology mandated an immediate home visit, satisfying the criteria that care could not be safely deferred until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to withhold Perindopril immediately. Recommended a trial of a simple linctus or honey-based soothing agent for pharyngeal mechanical relief. Future labs: basic metabolic panel upon switching to an Angiotensin II Receptor Blocker.
Positive Behavioral Change: Patient instructed to sleep with the head of the bed elevated using two extra pillows. By physically altering sleep mechanics, gravity reduces pooling of secretions and upper airway sensitivity. The reward is uninterrupted sleep intervals, reinforcing the behavior until the medication washes out.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: General Practice review in 48 hours for blood pressure check and prescription of an alternative antihypertensive agent.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden shortness of breath at rest, chest heaviness, hemoptysis, or high fever, they must present to the nearest Emergency Department or call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal investigations and resolution upon medication cessation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expected resolution of symptoms within one to four weeks following the discontinuation of the offending medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you experience breathing difficulties at rest, cough up blood, or develop severe chest pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The blood pressure medication you recently started alters a chemical pathway that causes a buildup of a protein in the lungs, triggering the cough reflex. Stopping the drug is the only effective treatment.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the Perindopril immediately. Prop yourself up with extra pillows tonight to minimize throat irritation.

